AUTHORS = f"""# Getting a database — ce-pg for app authors

You need `ce-pg` installed somewhere on the mesh. It does not have to be your node —
you call a capability, not a machine.

## Vendor an SDK

Copy one file next to your app, alongside the `ce.py` node client:

- Python: `sdk/py/cepg.py` from {REPO}
- JavaScript / TypeScript: `sdk/js/cepg.js` (browser or Node 18+)

## Declare your storage at startup

```python
import ce
from cepg import Postgres

pg = Postgres(ce.connect().wait_ready())
db = pg.ensure("myapp", migrations_dir="migrations")
```

`migrations_dir` applies every `*.sql` file in filename order, each named after its
file, so `001-init.sql`, `002-add-index.sql` is a complete, ordered, immutable schema
history with no extra bookkeeping. Call it on every boot — it is idempotent.

A migration is **immutable**. Re-applying a name with different SQL is refused, because
at that moment the code in front of you and the schema behind you disagree, and
continuing is how a deploy corrupts a database it thinks it understands.

## Read and write

```python
db.exec("insert into notes(body, tags) values ($1, $2)", ["hello", {{"k": 1}}])
db.rows("select id, body from notes where body like $1", ["%mesh%"])   # list of dicts
db.one("select * from notes where id = $1", [7])                        # dict or None
db.value("select count(*) from notes")                                  # scalar
db.tx([("update a set n = n + 1", []), ("insert into b(x) values ($1)", [2])])
```

Always pass values as `params` with `$1`/`$2` placeholders. The client binds them
through the protocol's Parse/Bind; a string-built statement throws that away and gives
you an injection.

`query`/`rows`/`one`/`value` are **read-only, enforced by the server** — they run inside
`BEGIN READ ONLY`, so a write comes back as SQLSTATE `25006`. Writes go through `exec`
or `tx`.

## What you get back

Integers, floats, booleans, `json`/`jsonb`, `NULL` and `bytea` (as `{{"base64": ...}}`)
arrive as native values. Everything else — `numeric`, timestamps, arrays, ranges,
extension types — arrives as **the exact string postgres produced**. A numeric squeezed
through a float is a money bug, and an invented array parser is a bug that hides for a
year.

## If you really need a native connection

`db.dsn()` returns a real connection string, gated separately by `pg:dsn:<db>`. It
bypasses every check in ce-pg, permanently, for whoever ends up holding it — so ask for
it only when a native driver or a bulk load genuinely requires it, and use `db.rotate()`
to invalidate one.

## Errors

React to `sqlstate`, never to message text: `23505` is a unique violation, `42P01` an
undefined table, `25006` a write attempted through a read-only op. The Python SDK raises
`PgError` carrying `sqlstate`, `detail` and `hint`.

Parent: [[ce-pg — postgres as a capability of the mesh]]
"""
upsert("Getting a database — ce-pg for app authors", AUTHORS, parent)

API = f"""# The pg-ctl API and its capabilities

Service `ce.pg`, topic `pg/ctl`. Envelope in:
`{{"op": "...", "args": {{...}}, "cap": "<token>"}}`. Out: `{{"ok": true, "result": ...}}`
or `{{"error": "...", "sqlstate": "..."}}`.

Every skin calls these same ops and none is privileged: the SDKs, `pgctl.py`, the MCP
server, and the cell in [[ce-pg-web — the postgres cell]].

## Open — no capability

- `describe` — the full catalog: every op, its arguments, and the exact grant it needs.
  An agent that has never seen this app bootstraps from here.
- `ping` — liveness.
- `status` — cluster mode, version, up/down, pools, and which provisioning step is in
  flight. Paths are redacted without `pg:admin`.
- `server` — the postgres binaries this node holds `{{platform, version, cid}}`, so
  another node can pull them from the mesh instead of the internet. Open on purpose:
  this is how a node with no capabilities and no internet bootstraps a database.

## pg:admin — the cluster

- `up` — make the cluster exist and run; acquires a server if the host has none.
- `down` — stop it, and it stays stopped until `up` (the watchdog obeys).
- `install` — acquire a server now.
- `logs` — tail the postgres log.
- `ensure {{db, migrations?}}` — database + role + password + schema, idempotent.
- `db.list`, `db.drop {{db, confirm}}`, `db.rotate {{db}}`
- `backup {{db}}` / `restore {{db, cid}}` — `pg_dump` into a content-addressed node blob.

## pg:query:&lt;db&gt; — reading one database

- `query {{db, sql, params, limit?, as_objects?}}` — runs inside `BEGIN READ ONLY`.
- `db.info {{db}}` — tables with sizes and approximate row counts.
- `migrations {{db}}` — what has been applied.

## pg:exec:&lt;db&gt; — writing one database

- `exec {{db, sql, params}}` — a write.
- `tx {{db, statements}}` — all-or-nothing.
- `script {{db, sql}}` — several statements in one transaction (DDL; takes no params).
- `migrate {{db, name, sql}}` — apply one named migration, once ever.

## pg:dsn:&lt;db&gt; — the credentials

- `dsn {{db}}` — the native connection string. Separate from `pg:exec` on purpose: a DSN
  bypasses every check here for as long as the password is valid, for anyone holding it.

## Minting a grant

```
ce-iam grant --to <node-id> --action pg:query:myapp --nonce <n>
```

The enforced action carries the database, so a grant for one app's data can never be
replayed against another's, and a reader can never write. `ce-iam` cannot mint `pg:*` —
its wildcard expansion runs against a hardcoded action universe — so grants name exact
strings, which is the safer default anyway. See
[[Identity, capabilities, people and devices]].

The operator's own node self-issues a chain for its own databases into the `ce-iam`
wallet under the label `pg`, re-issued whenever a database is created, so local tools
work with no `--cap` juggling.

> [!warning] `CE_PG_AUTH=allow` opens the gate for a single-user dev box and says so in
> `describe`. `deny` refuses everything, which is how you prove the gate is wired.

Repo: {REPO} · Parent: [[ce-pg — postgres as a capability of the mesh]]
