INSTALL = f"""# How ce-pg installs postgres by itself

> [!quote] Leif, 2026-07-26: "you should never have to manually install it ce should do
> it for you. brew too if its really required. automatation..."

The directive this implements, verbatim and in full:
[[2026-07-26 · a ceapp installs the software it wraps — never make the operator do it]],
under [[Repos, packaging and distribution]]. ce-pg is its first worked example.

`ce app install ce-pg` ends with a working database on a machine that has no postgres,
no homebrew and no root. No second command, no flag, no instructions to follow. This
page is the pattern, because **any ceapp that wraps third-party software should carry
it**.

## The ladder, cheapest first

1. **host** — postgres is already installed. Search the real prefixes, not just `PATH`:
   a supervised daemon runs with almost no `PATH`, and homebrew keeps postgres keg-only
   under `opt/postgresql@NN/bin`. Both cases end with a perfectly installed server that
   the app swears does not exist. This route also brings the client tools.
2. **cached** — a portable server this app already unpacked, in its own data dir. A
   restart must never re-download; an app that fetches 30MB every boot is not installed,
   it is downloading.
3. **mesh** — another node running the same app already holds the binaries for this
   platform. Fetch the blob by CID. Content-addressed, so **the CID is the
   verification**.
4. **download** — a portable, self-contained build, unpacked into the app's own data
   dir, and then **published into the node blob store** so the next node takes step 3
   instead of the internet.
5. **package manager** — brew/apt/dnf/apk/pacman. Last resort, and the only step that
   can need root. With steps 1-4 it is nearly unreachable.

Steps 3 and 4 together are peer-to-peer software distribution working today, for
arbitrary upstream software, with no registry — see
[[Repos, packaging and distribution]]. The first node pays the download; every later
node pulls it from the mesh.

## Why portable rather than a package manager

The portable tree (`bin/`, `lib/`, `share/`) lives inside the app's own data dir and
runs from wherever it is unpacked. Nothing is installed system-wide and nothing needs
sudo — which is precisely why an unprivileged app can provision a database at all, on a
laptop or on a board. A package manager assumes root and assumes a package manager;
a fresh board has neither.

## Say what you are doing

Acquiring a server takes a minute or two. `status` reports the phase while it happens
(`acquiring: downloading postgres 16.14.0 for darwin-amd64 (95s)`), because an app that
reports "down, no binaries" while it is busy installing looks like a dead app.

## Known cost

The portable build ships the **server only** — no `pg_dump`, `pg_restore` or `psql`.
Every SQL op works regardless, because ce-pg speaks the wire protocol itself and needs
no client binaries; `backup`, `restore` and `psql` refuse with the reason and are picked
up automatically if the client tools are ever installed.

## Traps worth stealing

- **python's `tarfile` is quadratic on large archives.** Extracting member-by-member
  from a `.txz` re-decompresses the whole stream per member: 130MB wrote 3MB in EIGHT
  MINUTES and looked exactly like a hang. Use `mode="r|xz"`, one sequential pass — and
  do your own per-member path-traversal check, because the archive came off the network.
- **A supervised python daemon is GIL-bound during heavy work.** The same unpack took
  96s standalone and roughly 370s inside the running app.
- **Verify what you can, and say what you cannot.** The published digest beside the
  artifact catches a corrupted download; it is not supply-chain proof, because it comes
  from the same host. The mesh route has no such gap: the CID is the content.

The implementation is `serverpkg.py` in {REPO} — about 200 lines, standard library only.
Copy it. Any app that today expects a system package — a database, a media encoder, a
runtime — should carry this ladder instead, and every one that does makes the mesh a
little more able to install itself.

Parent: [[ce-pg — postgres as a capability of the mesh]]
"""
upsert("How ce-pg installs postgres by itself", INSTALL, parent)

DECISIONS = f"""# ce-pg — decisions, and the traps we hit

Why it is shaped this way, and what running it taught us. Every trap below is guarded by
a test that stays forever.

## Decisions

- **The wire protocol is spoken directly, standard library only.** A script ceapp that
  told its operator to `pip install psycopg2` would need a C toolchain and libpq headers
  on every board in the fleet. The protocol is small and stable: startup,
  SCRAM-SHA-256 with server-signature verification, MD5, TLS, the extended query
  protocol, pooling.
- **`query` is read-only because the SERVER says so**, not because anything inspects the
  SQL. It runs inside `BEGIN READ ONLY`. Classifying SQL by text is a filter, not a
  guarantee, and every regex that tries it has a bypass.
- **Parameters are always bound.** The client never concatenates a value into a
  statement.
- **Identifiers are validated AND quoted.** Database and role names cannot be bound
  parameters. The strict pattern is what makes it safe; the quoting is what keeps it
  safe when someone widens the pattern later.
- **`dsn` is a separate capability from `exec`.** Handing over a connection string hands
  over everything that role can do, permanently, with no further checks.
- **Numerics and timestamps stay exact strings.** A numeric squeezed through a float is
  a money bug.
- **Secrets are node-local, not replicated.** Per-database role passwords live in one
  `0600` file on the node that runs the cluster. They only mean anything to a process
  that can already reach that socket, and a secret that replicates has N times the blast
  radius. Apps do not need them anyway — they hold a capability.
- **No state app in the trio.** The state of a SQL database is the cluster itself, the
  one thing a database app cannot honestly keep as a CRDT. See
  [[App anatomy — State / Logic / Viz, docs, robustness]].

## Traps, all found by running it

- **`initdb` in place.** Interrupted halfway, it leaves a directory that passes every
  "is it initialised" check (`PG_VERSION` exists) but can never start, plus a lock file
  from its own standalone backend. It now builds beside the real path and renames — a
  rename cannot half-happen.
- **A stale `postmaster.pid` wedges the cluster forever.** `pg_ctl` refuses with
  "another server might be running" and has no `--force`. ce-pg clears a lock only when
  its owner is provably not a live postgres, identified by BINARY (`ps -o comm=`), never
  by command line.
- **Locale.** A supervised daemon inherits almost no environment, and postgres then
  refuses with "invalid locale settings" or, on macOS, dies with "postmaster became
  multithreaded during startup" — neither message mentions locales. ce-pg resolves a
  locale the host actually has and records it.
- **A suspended portal sends no CommandComplete.** Every limited query returned zero
  rows until the collector also emitted a result at `ReadyForQuery`.
- **A failed statement poisons a pooled connection** ("current transaction is aborted,
  commands ignored") until something rolls back. The next borrower paid for it.
- **`pg_restore` as superuser** hands the restored tables to the superuser and locks the
  app out of its own data. Restore passes `--role <owner>`.
- **`down` was a lie.** The watchdog restarted the cluster ten seconds later. Operator
  intent is a file now, so it survives a restart of the app, and only `up` clears it.
- **Creating a database is not a query.** It copies a template and fsyncs it: 24s
  measured on a freshly provisioned cluster here. A 30s statement timeout killed a
  perfectly normal `ensure`.
- **A read op must never do work.** `status` cost 1747ms because it re-globbed a dozen
  directories and spawned `postgres --version` on every call. Cached, its own work is
  now ~0.

## Measured, on this mac

- A mesh `ping` to an app doing NO work: min 86ms, median 413ms, max 1354ms, on a node
  with 66+ supervised apps. That is the node's delivery path, not the app — the same
  cost every app on this node pays. Against the 1.6 ms bar in
  [[The canvas and the UI layer]] it is 50-250x.
- `CREATE DATABASE` on a fresh cluster: 24s (fsync-bound). `strategy file_copy` was
  worse than the default `wal_log`.

Numbers like these — an app quietly getting slower, a resource nobody is watching — are
what [[Sentinel - suspicious-activity flagging on the mesh]] exists to notice before a
human does.

Repo: {REPO} · Parent: [[ce-pg — postgres as a capability of the mesh]]
"""
upsert("ce-pg — decisions, and the traps we hit", DECISIONS, parent)

